    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and processes the *same* token sequence simultaneously.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a subset of experts; different tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn practice, large MoE models (e.g., DeepSeek-V3, Mixtral-class architectures) combine both: attention and shared (dense) layers use TP, while the MoE feed-forward blocks use EP. The combination is sometimes called **PTD-P** or simply \"TP+EP\" sharding.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a linear layer `Y = XW` where `X \u2208 \u211d^{B\u00d7d}` (batch \u00d7 hidden) and `W \u2208 \u211d^{d\u00d7h}`, distributed over `P` devices.\n\nTwo canonical splits:\n\n**Column-parallel (weight split along output dim):**\n- Each device holds `W_i \u2208 \u211d^{d \u00d7 h/P}`.\n- Each device computes `Y_i = X \u00b7 W_i \u2208 \u211d^{B \u00d7 h/P}` \u2014 no communication needed for the matmul itself.\n- Outputs are partial slices of the full output dimension.\n\n**Row-parallel (weight split along input dim):**\n- Each device holds `W_i \u2208 \u211d^{(d/P) \u00d7 h}`.\n- Input `X` must also be split along the feature dimension: each device gets `X_i \u2208 \u211d^{B \u00d7 d/P}`.\n- Each device computes a partial sum `Y_i",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
